How hidden fields are presented. (Their semantics, such as closedness and
package scope, are tested in internal/cuetil.)

- evaluated/raw/documented show hidden fields; final and data omit them.
  cue.Hidden(false) has no effect on evaluated.
- A field referring to a hidden sibling keeps the reference in evaluated
  (r: _h), so a comprehension under _h appears only under _h. final resolves it
  (referenced: {a: 2}).
- An embedded hidden field stays an embedding in evaluated ({_h, _h: ...}),
  though its fields surface in the embedding struct once they exist.
- Selecting into a hidden field's struct (selected) shows its comprehension at
  the top.
- Hidden labels are package-scoped, but the text shows only the bare name: a
  hidden field of another package prints the same as one of this package.
-- in.cue --
input: _
_hidden: {if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}
h: {_h: {if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}, r: _h}

cases: {
	plain:       {a: 1, _h: 2}
	referenced:  {a: _h, _h: 2}
	comprehensionUnderHidden: h
	selected:    h.r
	viaOutsideHidden: {k: _hidden}
	embedded:    {_h: {if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}, _h}
	#D: {a: int, _h: a}
	inDefinition: #D
}
-- conjunct 0 --
plain: {a: 1, _h: 2}
## referenced
-- conjunct 0 --
referenced: {a: _h, _h: 2}
## comprehensionUnderHidden
-- conjunct 0 --
<nil>
-- conjunct 1 --
h
## selected
-- conjunct 0 --
h
-- conjunct 1 --
<nil>
## viaOutsideHidden
-- conjunct 0 --
viaOutsideHidden: {k: _hidden}
## embedded
-- conjunct 0 --
_h
-- conjunct 1 --
<nil>
## inDefinition
-- conjunct 0 --
<nil>
-- conjunct 1 --
#D
-- conjunct 0 --
plain: {a: 1, _h: 2}
## referenced
-- conjunct 0 --
